        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Mesoamerican religion</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 1200 BCE – 1697 CE</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Origin</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a>, from central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> to Honduras, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olmecs">Olmec civilisation</a> from about 1200 BCE</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Founder or early teachers</span><span class="info-stat-val">None: it was shared and transformed over three thousand years by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olmecs">Olmecs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zapotecs">Zapotecs</a>, Maya, Teotihuacanos, Toltecs, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mixtecs">Mixtecs</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztecs</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Scriptures</span><span class="info-stat-val">Painted screenfold books, of which only a handful survive, such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a> Codex and the Codex Borgia; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/popol-vuh">Popol Vuh</a> of the K’iche' Maya, written down in the 16th century</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Followers today</span><span class="info-stat-val">None as a public religion since the Spanish conquest; Maya day-keepers still count the 260-day calendar, and many old traditions live on within Catholic practice</span></div>
