    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>For three thousand years the peoples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a>, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olmecs">Olmecs</a> to the Maya and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztecs</a>, shared a religion of gods of rain, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a>, sun and wind, of sacred mountains and caves, and of time measured by interlocking calendars.</li><li>Kings and priests offered the gods <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/incense">incense</a>, their own blood and, at times, human lives in pyramid temples, to repay the gods who, in the myths, had sacrificed themselves to set the sun moving and to create humanity.</li><li>Suppressed after the Spanish conquest, it survives in a handful of painted books, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/popol-vuh">Popol Vuh</a>, the ruins of its temple cities and traditions, such as the 260-day calendar, still kept in Maya communities.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In 1562 the Franciscan friar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diego-de-landa">Diego de Landa</a> burned the Maya books he found at Maní in Yucatán as works of the devil; only four Maya books survive today, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

